## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.x · **.NET 10** · **Integration-test calibration applied** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4** (of 7)   |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← hard 3-second wall-clock wait
```

Even in integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` introduces non-determinism and slows the suite. The method is `async` yet uses a synchronous blocking sleep instead of polling or a signal.

**Fix — poll with timeout:**
```csharp
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
{
    if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id)) break;
    await Task.Delay(50);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
    "Notification not sent within timeout");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures from timing jitter; 3 s of dead wall-clock time per run.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

One branch is never executed for a given run — this hides a testing gap. You're verifying different properties depending on runtime type, so the test effectively tests half its intent.

**Fix — split into two tests with explicit setup:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", 0.15m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silent coverage gap — whichever branch *doesn't* execute is permanently untested.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    // ← no assertions
}
```

The test passes even if every insert silently fails (e.g., rows never committed). Framework reports "green" regardless.

**Fix — add a verification assertion:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "Expected all 100 bulk users persisted");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage metrics look healthy but zero behavior is verified.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, none with a message parameter. When one fails in CI, the stack trace only shows the line number — not *which property* was wrong.

**Fix — add assertion messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-assigned");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis in CI — especially when multiple assertions share the same failure mode.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-designed:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear Arrange-Act-Assert, focused scope |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, negative assertion (`IsNull`) proves absence |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion is contextually obvious (3 inserted → 3 expected); `CollectionAssert` adds depth |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Realistic concurrency scenario with a concrete correctness assertion |

The in-memory SQLite approach avoids the **Mystery Guest** smell entirely — no external file/server dependency. The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` setup is used by *all* tests, so there is no **General Fixture** over-breadth.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Why first |
|----------|-------|-----------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Prevents false-pass; highest risk |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Eliminates a hidden gap |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Reduces flakiness + 3 s runtime |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | ~2 min | Improves diagnosis speed |

**Bottom line:** 3 of 7 tests have high-severity smells that could mask real bugs. The other 4 tests are solid — well-structured integration tests using in-memory Sqlite with good isolation. Fixing the top 3 issues takes ~15 minutes and meaningfully improves suite reliability.
